page("smores-and-grilled-desserts-compact-grill","S'mores and Grilled Desserts on a Compact Grill","Use the last coals for s'mores, grilled peaches, pineapple, banana boats and griddle cobbler. Plus using the Slot Grill Compact as a mini fire pit where allowed.",IMG["c13"],recipe2(
"<p>The best part of a charcoal cook might be what happens after dinner. As the coals die down, they're perfect for desserts: gentle heat for toasting marshmallows, caramelizing fruit, and warming chocolate. Where fires are allowed, the Slot Grill Compact also works as a small fire pit for marshmallows. One RV owner described setting it up first thing in the morning for a \"sweet breakfast.\"</p><blockquote>\"This morning we wanted to roast marshmallows. Set up was efficient. In minutes we were roasting our sweet breakfast.\"<cite>Richard Shapero, Compact owner</cite></blockquote>",
"4","About 15–30 minutes",
["S'mores: graham crackers, chocolate bars, large marshmallows, roasting sticks","Grilled peaches: 2 ripe peaches halved and pitted, 1 tablespoon melted butter, 1 tablespoon brown sugar, pinch of cinnamon","Grilled pineapple: fresh pineapple spears, honey or brown sugar","Banana boats: bananas, chocolate chips, mini marshmallows, foil","Vanilla ice cream or whipped cream (optional)"],
["<b>Use the dying coals.</b> After the main cook, let the fire settle to a gentle glow. Pull the grates if roasting marshmallows directly.","<b>S'mores:</b> hold a marshmallow a few inches above the glowing coals, turning slowly until golden. For a quick char, get closer, but watch it. Sandwich with chocolate between graham crackers; let the hot marshmallow melt the chocolate.","<b>Grilled peaches:</b> brush cut sides with butter, place cut-side down on a clean, oiled grate over medium coals for 3–5 minutes until marked. Flip, sprinkle with brown sugar and cinnamon, and cook 2–3 minutes until soft.","<b>Grilled pineapple:</b> grill spears 2–3 minutes per side over medium heat, brushing with honey at the end.","<b>Banana boats:</b> slit bananas lengthwise through the peel (not all the way), stuff with chocolate and marshmallows, wrap in foil, and set on the grate over low coals for 5–10 minutes.","<b>Serve</b> warm, with ice cream if you have a cooler."],
why="<ul><li>Small coal bed = gentle, controllable heat as it dies down.</li><li>The <a href='"+GRID+"'>griddle</a> is great for warming fruit and toasting pound cake.</li><li>Doubles as a small fire pit where allowed.</li></ul>",
variations=["<b>Peanut butter cup s'mores:</b> swap the chocolate bar for peanut butter cups.","<b>Griddle cobbler:</b> sliced fruit, butter and brown sugar on the griddle, topped with crumbled cookies.","<b>Grilled pound cake:</b> butter slices and toast on the griddle; top with fruit.","<b>Cinnamon apples:</b> sliced apples with butter, cinnamon and sugar in a foil packet."],
trouble=[("Marshmallows catch fire","Too close to hot coals. Raise them and turn slowly."),("Fruit sticks","Grate not clean and oiled, or fruit moved too early."),("Chocolate won't melt","Warm the cracker and chocolate briefly on the griddle.")],
serve="<p>Make sure kids roasting marshmallows stay back from the grill and use long sticks. When the coals are out and cold, dispose of ash safely.</p>",fire=False),"recipes",
[("Can you make s'mores on a charcoal grill?","Yes. Toast marshmallows over the glowing coals after the main cook, with the grates removed or above them."),("Can I use the Slot Grill Compact as a fire pit?","Where fires are allowed, owners use it as a small fire pit for marshmallows. Follow local fire rules and burn bans."),("What fruit grills well?","Peaches, pineapple, bananas, apples and plums all grill well over medium coals."),("How do I keep fruit from sticking?","Clean and oil the grate, use medium heat, and let the fruit sear before moving it.")])

page("grilled-fish-compact-grill","Grilled Fish on a Small Charcoal Grill","Grill fish fillets, steaks and whole fish on the Slot Grill Compact: which fish hold up, the griddle method, foil packets, and campside catch cooking.",IMG["pl2"],recipe2(
"<p>Fish has a reputation for sticking and falling apart on grills. On a small charcoal grill, the easiest way to avoid that is to choose firm fish, dry it well, and use the <a href='"+GRID+"'>Slot Grill griddle (plancha)</a> for delicate fillets. Firm steaks and whole fish can go right on a hot, clean, oiled grate. It's a perfect match for <a href='/fishing-and-river-trip-grilling.html'>fishing and river trips</a>, where you can cook what you catch (where it's legal to keep).</p>",
"2","About 20–25 minutes",
["2 fish fillets or steaks (6–8 oz each), about 1\" thick, skin on if available. Firm choices: salmon, swordfish, tuna, mahi-mahi, redfish, snapper. Delicate fish (tilapia, flounder, trout fillets) are best on the griddle or in foil.","2 tablespoons oil","Salt and pepper","Lemon, butter, garlic, fresh dill or parsley","Optional: blackening seasoning"],
["<b>Dry</b> fish thoroughly with paper towels.","<b>Oil the fish</b> (not just the grate) and season.","<b>Light a chimney</b> and build a hot zone with a cooler zone.","<b>Preheat</b> the grate or griddle well; scrape and oil.","<b>Cook skin-side down first</b> over medium-high heat. Leave it alone until it releases on its own, usually 3–5 minutes.","<b>Flip once</b> with a wide spatula and cook another 2–4 minutes. A good rule of thumb is about 8–10 minutes per inch of thickness total.","<b>Check doneness:</b> 145°F, or flesh opaque and flakes easily with a fork. Many people cook salmon and tuna to lower temperatures by preference.","<b>Finish</b> with lemon, butter, garlic and herbs."],
extra="<h2>Foil packet method (for delicate fish)</h2><ol><li>Lay a fillet on heavy-duty foil with sliced lemon, butter, garlic, herbs, salt and pepper. Add thin-sliced vegetables if you like.</li><li>Seal tightly, leaving some room for steam.</li><li>Cook on the grate over medium heat about 10–15 minutes, depending on thickness.</li><li>Open carefully (steam) and check that the fish flakes.</li></ol><h2>Whole fish at camp</h2><p>Clean and scale a small whole fish, stuff the cavity with lemon and herbs, oil the skin well, and cook over medium heat on a clean grate or griddle, turning once carefully, until the flesh by the backbone is opaque. Follow local fishing regulations and handle catch on ice.</p>",
why="<ul><li>Griddle = no sticking, no falling through.</li><li>Compact size matches a couple of fillets or a small catch.</li><li>Fast cook, small fire.</li></ul>",
variations=["<b>Blackened redfish or mahi:</b> coat in blackening seasoning and sear on a very hot griddle.","<b>Fish tacos:</b> flaked with slaw, lime and crema in warm tortillas.","<b>Gentle griddle finish:</b> sear thick fillets over the coals, then slide the griddle side over the cool zone to finish."],
trouble=[("Fish sticks","Fish wet, grate cold or dirty, or flipped too early. Dry, oil, preheat, wait for release."),("Falls apart","Use firmer fish, a wide spatula, the griddle, or foil."),("Dry fish","Overcooked; pull as soon as it flakes.")],
serve="<p>Keep fish on ice until it hits the grill. Clean the griddle while warm and oil it once cool; fish odors fade with heat and a scrub.</p>"),"recipes",
[("What fish is best for grilling?","Firm fish like salmon, swordfish, tuna, mahi-mahi, redfish and snapper hold up on a grate. Delicate fish are best on a griddle or in foil."),("How do I keep fish from sticking to the grill?","Dry the fish, oil it, preheat and clean the grate, and don't flip until it releases on its own. A griddle plate makes it easier."),("What temperature should fish be cooked to?","USDA recommends 145°F or until the flesh is opaque and separates easily with a fork."),("How long does fish take on a grill?","A rough rule is 8 to 10 minutes total per inch of thickness over medium-high heat.")])
